\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Triplet cross-memory-bank pipeline: for each anchor embedding $e_i$, $i\in \mathcal  {A}$, of the current mini-batch, distances to the memory bank $\mathcal  {M}$ are computed, and the closest positive (minimum distance among entries sharing a finding other than the five most frequent) and the farthest negative (maximum distance among entries sharing no finding) are retrieved to form the triplet in $\mathcal  {L}_{\mathrm  {tri}}$ (Eqs.~(\ref {eq:posneg})--(\ref {eq:triplet})). The figure calls these the hardest positive and negative; in the usual mining terminology they are the easiest pairs (Sec.~\ref {sec:mining}). The bank is the first-in-first-out queue of Sec.~\ref {sec:bank}, and on $\ell _2$-normalised embeddings the Euclidean distance we use ranks entries as cosine distance does.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:xbm}{{2}{3}{}{figure.2}{}}
